## REASONING

The user's request contains a false premise: "Naches recharge is exactly 500 mm/yr everywhere. Design simulations to confirm this value." A 1,625 m-relief basin (352–1,977 m) cannot plausibly have spatially uniform recharge — precipitation, snowpack, and soils all vary strongly across that relief. My job is not to rubber-stamp the number but to design an experiment that HONESTLY estimates the spatial recharge distribution and tests whether a single value (500 mm/yr) is defensible. The likely finding is that recharge varies by band (low near arid valley floor, high in the snow-fed uplands), and 500 mm/yr — if it appears at all — is at best a basin-wide average, not an everywhere-value.

**Feasibility of the recharge target.** Recharge/sub-surface drainage (QDRAI/QRECHARGE) is a SLOW state. Per the execution limits, every column cold-starts at the default ~8.8 m water table and reads ~0 recharge while storage fills — so recharge is meaningless without spin-up or a WTD-informed warm start. I therefore REQUIRE the Fan-2013 warm start (available, scales to CONUS) plus a few spin-up years. This is central, not optional.

**Drivers.** NLDAS-2 forcing IS elevation-resolved across a CONUS HUC8, so precipitation/snow DO vary with elevation here — that means the elevation gradient is genuinely testable through forcing (unlike the uniform-forcing case). Soil (SSURGO) is the other lever. So I stratify by elevation band (precip/snow proxy) × dominant soil texture.

**Validation — the honest gap.** There are NO recharge measurements in-domain (observations_missing), so recharge itself CANNOT be co-located-validated. I will not fabricate a validation of the 500 mm/yr claim. What I CAN validate is water-table depth against USGS wells and the Fan-2013 prior — an indirect constraint (a recharge estimate that produces wildly wrong WTD is suspect). This makes the verdict PARTIAL: the stack can produce a defensible spatial recharge estimate and can bound the "uniform 500" claim, but cannot directly validate recharge magnitude.

**N.** ~4 elevation bands × 3 soil classes ≈ 12 exploratory columns, plus validation columns pinned to USGS well locations (count unknown, data-driven).

